    "narrative": "For LawDog today, **Dylan Beavers** (68.38) earns the title of our strongest play, benefiting from a high-upside matchup against Sugano. The combination of a favorable hitter\u2019s park (+14%) and the SP\u2019s weak xERA (4.89) creates a perfect storm for production, even with the modest penalty for batting seventh. **Bryce Harper** (66.95) and **Zack Gelof** (66.86) follow closely; **Gelof** is particularly noteworthy, as his strong score is driven almost entirely by the massive 19% boost from opposing pitcher Bradford\u2019s poor metrics and a highly advantageous lineup position at the two-hole. While **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (48.68) remains a core piece of our roster, he is our most fragile start today, as facing an elite arm in Cease (2.97 xERA) and poor historical BvP splits significantly cap his daily ceiling.\n\nIn terms of bench decisions, **Jonathan Aranda** (47.17) was the last man out. He was suppressed by a poor park factor and a tough draw against Hagenman, which ultimately pushed him below the efficiency floor compared to the surging **Jung Hoo Lee** (64.17) and **Daulton Varsho** (60.87). Both **Lee** and **Varsho** benefit heavily from today's dynamic platoon advantages against right-handed pitching, which remains our primary lever for maximizing output. We are also keeping a close watch on **Lawrence Butler** (60.01); despite a 13% drag due to his platoon split, his spot in the lineup and the weakness of Bradford keep him firmly in the UTIL slot to chase volume.\n\nRisks remain focused on the lower end of the utility spectrum. We are avoiding **Tyler Freeman** entirely as he remains in the minors, and **Will Smith** is sitting for rest, leaving our depth charts thin for late-game adjustments. While we are aggressively targeting the weak xERA of Bradford and Sugano, we must be prepared for volatility from **Braden Montgomery** (48.82), who is hovering near our marginal threshold and requires a clean game to justify his start over our internal bench depth.",
